From: Ezurio
Subject: [PATCH] btattach: Use cfsetspeed() instead of OR-ing baud into c_cflag

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [not submitted upstream]

open_serial() sets the port speed by OR-ing the Bxxxx constant returned
by tty_get_speed() directly into ti.c_cflag. This relies on the Bxxxx
macros being small kernel CBAUD-encoded bit patterns, which was true
for older glibc.

Newer glibc (the "sane speed_t" termios rework) defines the public
Bxxxx macros as the literal baud rate value instead (e.g. B3000000 is
3000000), and instead relies on cfsetspeed()/tcsetattr() to translate
that value to the correct CBAUD bits or to BOTHER/termios2 for rates
that have no CBAUD encoding. OR-ing such a value straight into c_cflag
corrupts the c_cflag word instead of selecting a baud rate, so btattach
silently opens the port at the wrong baud rate at runtime.

Use cfsetspeed() so the C library handles the encoding correctly with
both old and new glibc.

diff --git a/tools/btattach.c b/tools/btattach.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/tools/btattach.c
+++ b/tools/btattach.c
@@ -46,7 +46,13 @@ static int open_serial(const char *path, unsigned int speed, bool flowctl)
 	memset(&ti, 0, sizeof(ti));
 	cfmakeraw(&ti);
 
-	ti.c_cflag |= (speed | CLOCAL | CREAD);
+	ti.c_cflag |= (CLOCAL | CREAD);
+
+	if (cfsetspeed(&ti, speed) < 0) {
+		perror("Failed to set speed");
+		close(fd);
+		return -1;
+	}
 
 	if (flowctl) {
 		/* Set flow control */
